.\" Title: vrw-list-commands
.\" Author: vrw contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrw-list-commands.1

.TH VRW\-LIST\-COMMANDS 1 "2025" "vrw" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrw\-list\-commands \- list running commands in machine-readable format

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrw
\fBlist\-commands\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
List all commands running inside vrw instances in a compact, tab-separated (TSV) format suitable for parsing in scripts and automated tools. This is the machine-friendly alternative to \fBvrw list\fR, which produces human-readable formatted output.

Unlike \fBvrw list\-vrw\fR (which lists instances), this subcommand lists the individual commands within each instance. It connects to each instance over HTTP to retrieve its command list and emits one line per command.

Each line after the header represents one command, with fields separated by tab characters. The header line is always emitted. Arguments are emitted as a JSON array string. Unreachable instances are silently skipped.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR "\-\-target " \fIPID\fR
Only list commands from the vrw instance with the given PID. This is a global option specified before the subcommand: \fBvrw \-\-target 12345 list\-commands\fR.

.SH OUTPUT FORMAT

Tab-separated fields:

.TP
.B VRW_PID
Process ID of the vrw instance hosting the command.

.TP
.B CMD_PID
OS process ID of the command itself.

.TP
.B NAME
Command binary name (e.g., \fBhtop\fR, \fBvim\fR, \fBcargo\fR).

.TP
.B ARGS
Command arguments as a JSON array string (e.g., \fB["notes.txt"]\fR, \fB[]\fR).

.TP
.B CERT
Certificate binding for the command, or \fB\-\fR if none.

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Basic usage:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrw list\-commands\fR
VRW_PID      CMD_PID NAME    ARGS                    CERT
12345           5678    htop     []                      -
12345           5679    vim     ["notes.txt"]            my-app
.fi
.RE

.PP
Find commands bound to a specific certificate:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrw list-commands | awk -F'\\t' '$5=="my-app" {print $2, $3}'\fR
5679 vim
.fi
.RE

.PP
Count total running commands:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrw list-commands | tail -n +2 | wc -l\fR
2
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Successful listing (including empty results).

.TP
.B 1
Error (registry access failure, invalid \fB\-\-target\fR PID, etc.).

.SH SEE ALSO

